-- | -------------- | ------------ |\n| **Rocky Linux 9** | ≥5.14.0            | 9.5            | fuse3-3.10.2 |","locale":"en","table":"table-0"},"1944":{"id":"/docs/Deploy/system-requirements-8","page_id":"/docs/Deploy/system-requirements","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Deploy/system-requirements#officially-supported-linux-distributions","content":"| OS Distribution | Kernel Requirement | Tested Version | Dependencies |\n| --------------- | ------------------ | -------------- | ------------ |\n| **RHEL 9**      | ≥5.14.0            | 9.5            | fuse3-3.10.2 |","locale":"en","table":"table-0"},"1945":{"id":"/docs/Deploy/system-requirements-9","page_id":"/docs/Deploy/system-requirements","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Deploy/system-requirements#officially-supported-linux-distributions","content":"| OS Distribution | Kernel Requirement | Tested Version | Dependencies |\n| --------------- | ------------------ | -------------- | ------------ |\n| **Ubuntu 22**   | ≥5.15.0            | 22.4           | fuse3-3.10.5 |","locale":"en","table":"table-0"},"1946":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","type":"page","content":"Technical Architecture","breadcrumbs":["Documents","Overview"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/Overview/architecture","locale":"en"},"1947":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-0","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"Architecture Overview","locale":"en"},"1948":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-1","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#high-performance-design","content":"High-Performance Design","locale":"en"},"1949":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-2","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#high-availability-design","content":"High Availability Design","locale":"en"},"1950":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-3","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture","content":"This chapter provides an in-depth introduction to Curvine's technical architecture, detailing the functionality, interaction patterns, and design principles of each layer component to help you comprehensively understand how the Curvine system works.","locale":"en"},"1951":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-4","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"Curvine adopts a layered distributed architecture design that ensures clear component responsibilities with excellent scalability and high availability. The entire architecture is primarily divided into three layers: **control layer** (Master), **storage layer** (Workers), and **access layer** (clients: FUSE, SDK, CLI), which collaborate to complete data storage, processing, and management tasks.","locale":"en"},"1952":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-5","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"Curvine consists of three main server-side roles plus clients and UFS:","locale":"en"},"1953":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-6","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**Curvine Client**: Data read/write operations are implemented by the client, which calls Curvine server-side interfaces through RPC (metadata from Master, block data from Workers). The client supports multiple access methods:","locale":"en"},"1954":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-7","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**FUSE Mount**: POSIX-compatible mount via libfuse/3 (curvine-fuse)","locale":"en"},"1955":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-8","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**HDFS & SDK**: Java / Python / libsdk interfaces","locale":"en"},"1956":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-9","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**CSI Driver**: Kubernetes volume driver (curvine-csi)","locale":"en"},"1957":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-10","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**CLI (`cv`)**: Admin tools for fs ops, report, and cluster management","locale":"en"},"1958":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-11","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"Internally, the client layer is composed of:","locale":"en"},"1959":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-12","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**UnifiedFileSystem**: Provides unified namespace with CurvineFS + UFS fallback","locale":"en"},"1960":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-13","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**FsReader / FsWriter**: Parallel, buffered I/O with zero-copy optimization","locale":"en"},"1961":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-14","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**BlockClient Pool**: Manages block_reader / block_writer for local and remote access","locale":"en"},"1962":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-15","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**Mount & Meta Cache**: Caches mount info and inode metadata","locale":"en"},"1963":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-16","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**Master**: The core control node; responsible for metadata (directory tree, file inodes, block locations), Worker registration, block allocation, and UFS mount table. Master can run as multiple nodes forming a Raft group for high availability; only the Raft leader serves metadata writes.","locale":"en"},"1964":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-17","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"The Master cluster mainly includes:","locale":"en"},"1965":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-18","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**MasterServer**: RPC handling and routing","locale":"en"},"1966":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-19","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**MasterFilesystem**: File system operations (mkdir, create, rename, delete, list)","locale":"en"},"1967":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-20","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**FsDir + InodeStore**: In-memory inode tree and metadata management","locale":"en"},"1968":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-21","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**JournalSystem**: Edit log and snapshot for recovery","locale":"en"},"1969":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-22","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**Worker / Mount Manager**: Worker heartbeat and UFS mount management","locale":"en"},"1970":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-23","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**Quota / Job / Replication**: Quota control, TTL, eviction, replication scheduling","locale":"en"},"1971":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-24","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**RocksDB Metadata Store**: Persistent metadata storage","locale":"en"},"1972":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-25","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**Raft Consensus**: Leader election and log replication","locale":"en"},"1973":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-26","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**Worker**: Stores block data and serves block read/write RPC; reports to Master via heartbeat. Does not hold file system metadata.","locale":"en"},"1974":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-27","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"Each Worker node includes:","locale":"en"},"1975":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-28","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**WorkerServer**: Handles block I/O RPC and heartbeat","locale":"en"},"1976":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-29","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**BlockStore**: Manages block lifecycle and local storage","locale":"en"},"1977":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-30","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**IO Handlers**: Read / Write pipeline with zero-copy","locale":"en"},"1978":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-31","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**Replication / Tasks**: Background replication and task scheduling","locale":"en"},"1979":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-32","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"Workers support **multi-tier storage**:","locale":"en"},"1980":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-33","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**MEM**: In-memory tier (< 100 ns)","locale":"en"},"1981":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-34","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**SSD**: NVMe / SATA (< 100 µs)","locale":"en"},"1982":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-35","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**HDD**: Capacity tier (< 10 ms)","locale":"en"},"1983":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-36","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**SPDK**: User-space NVMe acceleration","locale":"en"},"1984":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-37","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**UFS**: Underlying storage (S3, HDFS, etc.) accessed through Curvine’s data orchestration; Curvine provides a unified file system view over mounted UFS paths and native paths.","locale":"en"},"1985":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-38","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"Supported UFS types include:","locale":"en"},"1986":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-39","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**S3 / MinIO**","locale":"en"},"1987":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-40","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**OSS**","locale":"en"},"1988":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-41","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**HDFS**","locale":"en"},"1989":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-42","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**Local FS**","locale":"en"},"1990":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-43","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**OpenDAL (Azure, GCS, etc.)**","locale":"en"},"1991":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-44","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"In addition, Curvine provides foundational components:","locale":"en"},"1992":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-45","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**orpc**: High-performance RPC framework (Tokio-based, zero-copy)","locale":"en"},"1993":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-46","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**curvine-web**: Admin UI and cluster management","locale":"en"},"1994":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-47","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"**Metrics**: Prometheus-based monitoring (master/worker/client, job, cache)","locale":"en"},"1995":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-48","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#architecture-overview","content":"For deployment topology and component roles, see Deployment Architecture. For internal data flow (journal, replay, client read/write), see Basic Architecture.","locale":"en"},"1996":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-49","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#high-performance-design","content":"To achieve high performance, high concurrency, and low resource consumption, Curvine adopts the following technologies and design principles:","locale":"en"},"1997":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-50","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#high-performance-design","content":"**Pure Rust Implementation**: Curvine is implemented entirely in Rust, avoiding performance bottlenecks and resource consumption of traditional languages while ensuring code safety and stability","locale":"en"},"1998":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-51","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#high-performance-design","content":"**High-Performance RPC Framework**: Curvine implements a custom RPC communication framework supporting efficient data transmission with asynchronous I/O and zero-copy implementation within the framework","locale":"en"},"1999":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-52","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#high-performance-design","content":"**Zero-Cost Abstractions**: Zero-cost abstraction design where core modules directly interface with underlying systems, avoiding unnecessary abstraction layers and improving system performance and resource utilization","locale":"en"},"2000":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-53","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#high-performance-design","content":"**Asynchronous I/O**: Asynchronous I/O design fully utilizes system resources, avoiding blocking waits and improving system concurrent processing capabilities","locale":"en"},"2001":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-54","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#high-performance-design","content":"**Zero-Copy**: Zero-copy design avoids data copying and memory allocation, reducing system memory usage and resource consumption","locale":"en"},"2002":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-55","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#high-availability-design","content":"Curvine adopts distributed architecture design with multi-replica mechanisms and failover mechanisms to ensure system high availability:","locale":"en"},"2003":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-56","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#high-availability-design","content":"**Raft Protocol**: Uses Raft protocol to implement distributed consistency, ensuring data consistency and reliability","locale":"en"},"2004":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-57","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#high-availability-design","content":"**Automatic Failover**: Automatic failover mechanism that switches to backup nodes when primary nodes fail, ensuring system high availability","locale":"en"},"2005":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-58","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#high-availability-design","content":"**Multi-Replica Mechanism**: Multi-replica mechanism ensures data redundancy backup, improving system reliability and fault tolerance","locale":"en"},"2006":{"id":"/docs/Overview/architecture-59","page_id":"/docs/Overview/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/architecture#high-availability-design","content":"**Snapshot Mechanism**: Lightweight snapshot mechanism for periodic data backup, improving system recovery speed and stability","locale":"en"},"2007":{"id":"/docs/Overview","page_id":"/docs/Overview","type":"page","content":"Overview","breadcrumbs":["Documents","Overview"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/Overview","locale":"en"},"2008":{"id":"/docs/Overview/introduction","page_id":"/docs/Overview/introduction","type":"page","content":"Curvine Overview","breadcrumbs":["Documents","Overview"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/Overview/introduction","locale":"en"},"2009":{"id":"/docs/Overview/introduction-0","page_id":"/docs/Overview/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Overview/introduction#core-features","content":"Core Features","locale":"en"},"2010":{"id":"/docs/Overview/introduction-1","page_id":"/docs/Overview/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Overview/introduction#use-cases","content":"Use Cases","locale":"en"},"2011":{"id":"/docs/Overview/introduction-2","page_id":"/docs/Overview/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Overview/introduction#performance","content":"Performance","locale":"en"},"2012":{"id":"/docs/Overview/introduction-3","page_id":"/docs/Overview/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Overview/introduction#-modular-architecture","content":"🧩 Modular Architecture","locale":"en"},"2013":{"id":"/docs/Overview/introduction-4","page_id":"/docs/Overview/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/introduction","content":"Curvine is a high-performance distributed cache system written in Rust and released under the Apache 2.0 license. It provides a unified cached file-system view over external storage systems, with low-latency metadata access, high-throughput data access, and POSIX-compatible FUSE integration.","locale":"en"},"2014":{"id":"/docs/Overview/introduction-5","page_id":"/docs/Overview/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/introduction","content":"Curvine uses a classic Master/Worker architecture. Master nodes manage metadata and cluster coordination, while Worker nodes store and serve data blocks. Metadata consistency and high availability are provided through Raft.","locale":"en"},"2015":{"id":"/docs/Overview/introduction-6","page_id":"/docs/Overview/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/introduction","content":"Curvine exposes multiple access paths for analytics, AI, and data infrastructure workloads, including the Rust CLI, FUSE, and Java/Python SDK bindings.","locale":"en"},"2016":{"id":"/docs/Overview/introduction-7","page_id":"/docs/Overview/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/introduction#core-features","content":"**Multi-cloud storage support**: Works with S3-compatible systems, HDFS, OSS, MinIO, and other UFS backends through a unified access layer","locale":"en"},"2017":{"id":"/docs/Overview/introduction-8","page_id":"/docs/Overview/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/introduction#core-features","content":"**Cloud-native integration**: Supports CSI-based deployment and Kubernetes-oriented packaging workflows","locale":"en"},"2018":{"id":"/docs/Overview/introduction-9","page_id":"/docs/Overview/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/introduction#core-features","content":"**Multi-tier cache**: Supports memory, SSD, and HDD cache strategies","locale":"en"},"2019":{"id":"/docs/Overview/introduction-10","page_id":"/docs/Overview/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Overview/introduction#core-features","content":"**POSIX-compatible access**: Provides a FUSE layer so existing applications can access cached data as a local file 