g back. Increase the budget based on the Worker's inventory size, up to the configured limit. Inventory collection runs outside the heartbeat task, and failed resync attempts back off."],[57,"The CacheRuntime `DataLoad` path has been verified with Fluid chart `helm-chart-fluid-1.1.0-alpha.10`, whose Fluid image tag is `v1.1.0-676f47a`. Older or custom Fluid charts may need the fallback notes in the DataLoad section."],[1379,"The chart creates `curvine-transfer` as an internal `ClusterIP` Service and\nwrites its Service FQDN to `[transfer].hostname`. Curvine infers the RPC\nendpoint from that hostname and `transfer.rpcPort`; do not configure\n`endpoints` in normal Helm deployments."],[1786,"The chart creates the `curvine-transfer` Deployment and Service, writes its\nService FQDN to `[transfer].hostname`, and derives the client endpoint from that\nhostname and `transfer.rpcPort`. Leave `endpoints` unset. An empty\n`transfer.storeUrl` uses the single-Pod SQLite default with a chart-managed PVC.\nSee Kubernetes Deployment\nfor the full value list."],[1460,"The chart keeps two master web ports: `master.webPort` (`9000`, the admin\nconsole, written to `[web].port`) and `master.web1Port` (`9001`, the master\nmetrics listener, written to `[master].web_port`). Standalone code defaults\ndiffer — admin console `9000`, master metrics `9010`, Worker `9002` — so always\nread the effective ports from the generated ConfigMap rather than assuming."],[65,"The class also defines `executionEntries.mountUFS`. Fluid calls this entry so Curvine can mount Dataset UFS paths before serving the Dataset. In the Curvine image, the script is:"],[67,"The class also defines `executionEntries.ReportSummary`. Fluid calls this entry from the master pod to refresh Dataset cache status. In the Curvine image, the script is:"],[61,"The class describes how Fluid starts each Curvine role:"],[3250,"The CLI can summarize the release-version distribution across all live\nworkers. This is the fastest way to see whether a cluster is running a\nhomogeneous set of versions or a mix of old and new workers:"],[1777,"The client address must be reachable from every CLI user and from every Worker\nthat may execute a Transfer task."],[2744,"The client attempts at most one follower. Connection and request time together are capped at the smaller of one second and half the remaining `client.rpc_timeout_ms` deadline. A retryable refusal or transport failure triggers one fallback through the leader route, using the remaining deadline. Failed peers enter a five-second cooldown shared by client clones. Terminal errors, such as file-not-found or permission errors, are returned directly rather than retried on the leader."],[1810,"The client configuration contains no database URL. The store belongs only to\nthe Transfer service."],[189,"The client is any component that uses the Curvine file system: **FUSE**, **Rust/Java/Python SDK**, or **CLI** (`cv`). It always talks to the **Master** for metadata and to **Workers** for block data."],[2794,"The client-level cache developed by Curvine itself caches more complete content than kernel cache:"],[3050,"The command line does not change when standalone Transfer is enabled:"],[3261,"The compatibility policy is configured in the master configuration file:"],[3370,"The Configuration page is a **read-only** view of the effective cluster\nconfiguration. It helps administrators understand and verify what the Master is\nactually running with; changing values still requires editing\n`curvine-cluster.toml` and restarting, or `cv admin config set` once the Master\nregisters runtime-adjustable keys."],[1763,"The configuration reference\nis the single source for configuration defaults. Deployment pages show only\nthe settings required for each topology."],[3307,"The console is the standalone `curvine-web` process, not part of the Master server. The Master's own `web_port` (`9010`) serves `/metrics` only."],[145,"the container is privileged when required by your cluster."],[2469,"The controller returns `VolumeContext` containing `master-addrs`, `fs-path`, and `curvine-path`."],[2468,"The controller validates StorageClass parameters and creates `curvine-path`."],[217,"The core design goals of the Curvine metadata architecture are clear and focused, mainly around four core requirements. First, it supports metadata storage for tens of millions to tens of billions of files and directories, adapting to mixed storage scenarios with massive small files and large files. Second, it ensures low latency in high-concurrency read/write scenarios, handling frequent high-frequency operations such as file creation, deletion, and modification. Third, it implements data consistency across multiple nodes in a distributed cluster, avoiding the problem that \"local changes are invisible to other nodes\". Fourth, it balances operational convenience and controllable costs, reducing the complexity of deployment, monitoring, and fault recovery."],[236,"The core logic is to accumulate a batch and then synchronize. The system merges metadata modification operations within a short time window, usually 1 to 10 milliseconds, or a certain number of operations, such as 100, into one batch log and then synchronizes it to other nodes in the cluster. The core benefit is reducing the number of log synchronizations and lowering the overhead of network transmission and disk writes. In high-concurrency scenarios, throughput can increase several times."],[248,"The core logic of Curvine's metadata architecture can be summarized as three layers of division of responsibilities, one trade-off, and one fallback. It ensures speed and massive storage while also considering data consistency and operational convenience:"],[227,"The core pain point of a distributed cluster is data inconsistency. For example, a file created on one machine may not be visible on another machine, affecting normal business operations. As the consistency hub of the Curvine distributed cluster, the Raft protocol is designed specifically to solve this problem. Its core mechanism is simple and efficient:"],[2830,"The core value of mounting is that after the mount is complete, regardless of whether the underlying storage is a local disk, S3 object storage, or HDFS, users only need one set of Curvine commands to operate it and do not need to care about differences between storage types."],[2589,"The CSI controller dynamically provisions a Curvine directory for each PVC."],[2590,"The CSI node plugin mounts each PVC into the matching pod."],[1880,"The CSI node plugin mounts volumes with FUSE, so the kind node needs `/dev/fuse`. kind nodes run as privileged containers and see the host's devices, so no extra mount is required. Check that the device is there:"],[963,"The current `ClusterConf` contains these sections:"],[1562,"The current `main` branch and release workflow are Linux-first. The officially documented tested distributions are:"],[3093,"The current implementation prints `curvine-cli <version>` together with commit / branch information."],[2975,"The current implementation strips `hostname:port` down to `hostname` before calling the decommission API. The port is mainly for readability and consistent input format."],[3009,"The current implementation uses `mount` for three related workflows:"],[2657,"The current read-side validation control is `--read-verify-ufs` on the mount:"],[459,"The current reference tree does not publish official throughput tables for these wrappers. Treat any throughput numbers as environment-specific measurements, not repository defaults."],[452,"The current Rust implementation supports only the four actions above. For each run it:"],[1588,"The current scripts do not use a `LOCAL_HOSTNAME` variable as the primary runtime input. When documenting deployment or debugging hostname issues, prefer the `CURVINE_*_HOSTNAME` variables above."],[2979,"The current source exposes these `fs` subcommands:"],[2945,"The current top-level commands in source are:"],[2346,"The current workflow uses the example manifests under `curvine-csi/examples` in the Curvine source tree:"],[102,"The Curvine CacheRuntimeClass sample uses an inline DataLoad script because Fluid runs data operations as short-lived Jobs. The script first generates Curvine config from the Fluid runtime JSON. If that file is not mounted, it falls back to Dataset metadata, pod labels, and finally a DataLoad name pattern such as `<dataset>-load`."],[32,"The Curvine Fluid materials live in the Curvine source tree:"],[2784,"The Curvine FUSE client can control kernel-level metadata cache through configuration. The default cache time is 1 second, which significantly improves query efficiency."],[2219,"The Curvine S3 gateway exposes a Curvine cluster through an S3-compatible HTTP API. Tools that already speak S3 — the AWS CLI, MinIO Client, boto3, or any S3 SDK — can read and write Curvine paths without FUSE or a native Curvine client. The gateway authenticates each request with AWS Signature V2/V4 and translates bucket and key names into Curvine filesystem operations."],[3221,"The Curvine team evaluates and either adds the version to `blocked_versions`\nor documents the incompatibility on this page."],[151,"the DataLoad command uses the wrong Curvine config or master address."],[107,"The DataLoad job is successful only if the Curvine load command exits successfully for every target path. If `cv load --watch` returns an error, the job should fail so Fluid can report the problem."],[665,"The default cluster configuration file is:"],[3556,"The default configuration is suitable for most scenarios. In high-load environments, you can adjust the following parameters:"],[2256,"The default listen address is `0.0.0.0:9900`. Override it and the region on the command line:"],[3519,"The default SQLite store is suitable for a single Transfer instance only."],[7112,"The default SQLite store is suitable for a single Transfer instance only."],[1587,"The defaults co