 `rclone` or similar tools that preserve `mtime`, imported files may immediately expire. Verify TTL settings before large-scale imports, or temporarily disable TTL."],[7077,"When using `rclone` or similar tools that preserve `mtime`, imported files may immediately expire. Verify TTL settings before large-scale imports, or temporarily disable TTL."],[2671,"When using tools such as `rclone` to copy files to Curvine, the tool may preserve the original modification time (`mtime`) of the source file. If the source file has existed for a long time, it may immediately trigger TTL expiration after being copied, causing the file to be deleted or freed."],[2660,"When validation fails, or when the cache misses, data is read directly from UFS. If automatic caching is enabled for that mount, Curvine can also submit a background load job. With `[transfer] enabled = true`, that job is owned by the standalone Transfer service; otherwise it uses the legacy Master job path."],[2900,"When writing after expiration, Curvine first copies the original data in UFS back to Curvine and then performs the write in Curvine. This is because most UFS backends, especially S3/OSS object storage, do not support standard POSIX features such as random writes, file holes, and incremental modification. Copying large files may take time and is expected behavior."],[730,"When you file a bug, include:"],[1746,"When you need to enable existing applications to access Curvine distributed cache without modification, FUSE can be used to mount Curvine as a local file system."],[869,"When you read the plan, put on your reviewer hat. If the scope feels wrong, say so now — it costs nothing at this stage."],[870,"When your commits are ready:"],[814,"Where to Follow Planning"],[5219,"Windows10+"],[5286,"windows下建议的git环境配置"],[390,"With `LocalFile` storage, the kernel's filesystem assigns disk blocks to each file and tracks free space. With SPDK, multiple Curvine blocks share the same raw bdev — there is no kernel file